x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-(4{\\pi }^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+8(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(4{\\pi }^{2}+3-8)-(-3+8) \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}-5-5 \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}-10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534510 true] [17592186292566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292567 13194139534510 true] [17592186292566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292568 13194139534510 true] [17592186292566 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534510 true] [17592186292566 1041 "field-1" 13194139534510 true] [17592186292566 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292567 13194139534510 true] [17592186292566 1110 #uuid "8b83b36d-4257-5c6d-8606-938e50d244b9" 13194139534510 true] [17592186292567 1034 "4\\pi^2-1